#---
# purpose: "closing fixture of the destructure-up-through-quicksort arc — `{lo, hi} = partition(...)` on the hot path of a recursive sort"
#---
#
# # quicksort
#
# The fixture that closes [[fz-fyq]]. Sorts `[3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5]`
# via classic divide-and-conquer:
#
# - `partition/4` splits the rest of the list around the pivot, returning a
#   `{lo, hi}` tuple of the two sub-lists.
# - `qsort/1` destructures that tuple — `{lo, hi} = partition(...)` — on
#   the hot path of every recursive call.
# - `append/2` glues the recursively-sorted halves back together.
#
# This is the program destructuring exists for: a tuple-returning helper
# whose two results need to flow into the next call, where without `=`-bind
# you would either write nested calls (`f(snd(partition(...)), fst(...))`)
# or build accessor helpers per arity. The fact that
# `{lo, hi} = partition(p, rest, [], [])` is one line, irrefutable, and
# folds to pure tuple projection in CLIF is the whole point.
#
# ## Notes
#
# `append/2` is body-recursive — fine for the 11-element demo input, would
# blow the stack on a million-element list. This fixture is a
# feature-coverage smoke, not a perf benchmark; the tail-recursive
# formulation is a worthwhile exercise but unrelated to what's being
# proved here.
#
# The `append`, `partition`, and `qsort` clauses all use the standard
# empty-list plus cons-list split. The two clauses of each are disjoint, so
# this fixture is also a regression against spurious `type/redundant-clause`
# warnings on list-total helpers.
#
# After printing the sorted list, this fixture prints the allocation counts and
# bytes `System.alloc_stats/0` reports for the value families (list cells,
# structs, closures, maps) and a `heap_bytes` headline computed from
# `:list_cons_bytes + :struct_bytes + :map_bytes`. The headline isolates
# immutable value heap objects from frame and scheduler details.
#
# Owned-cons reuse target for native JIT/AOT:
#
# - `list_cons_allocs = 11`
# - `list_cons_bytes = 176`
# - `struct_allocs = 0`
# - `struct_bytes = 0`
# - `closure_allocs = 0` and `closure_bytes = 0` on the interpreter; on the
#   native paths `closure_allocs = 2` and `closure_bytes = 64`, the only closures
#   being the two closures the scheduler builds to start the program
# - `continuation_allocs = 44` and `continuation_bytes = 2432` on the native
#   paths, 0 on the interpreter: every pending call to a function that needs
#   a frame of its own builds a heap frame that
#   is counted when allocated; a frame that resumes as the newest object is
#   given back, any other stays until the next collection
# - `map_allocs = 0`
# - `map_bytes = 0`
# - `heap_bytes = 176`
#
# Those numbers mean interpreter, JIT, and AOT allocate only the 11 literal
# input cons cells as values; the native paths also allocate continuation
# frames. All doors consume the same construction-owned retention.
def append([], ys), do: ys
def append([h | t], ys), do: [h | append(t, ys)]

def partition(_, [], lo, hi), do: {lo, hi}
def partition(p, [h | t], lo, hi) when h < p, do: partition(p, t, [h | lo], hi)
def partition(p, [h | t], lo, hi), do: partition(p, t, lo, [h | hi])

def qsort([]), do: []
def qsort([p | rest]) do
  {lo, hi} = partition(p, rest, [], [])
  append(qsort(lo), [p | qsort(hi)])
end

def main() do
  sorted = qsort([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6, 5, 3, 5])
  dbg(sorted)

  stats = System.alloc_stats()
  dbg(%{list_cons_allocs: stats[:list_cons_allocs], list_cons_bytes: stats[:list_cons_bytes], struct_allocs: stats[:struct_allocs], struct_bytes: stats[:struct_bytes], closure_allocs: stats[:closure_allocs], closure_bytes: stats[:closure_bytes], map_allocs: stats[:map_allocs], map_bytes: stats[:map_bytes]})
  heap_bytes = stats[:list_cons_bytes] + stats[:struct_bytes] + stats[:map_bytes]
  dbg(heap_bytes)
  dbg({stats[:continuation_allocs], stats[:continuation_bytes]})
end
